  exp: "Little's area on the anteroinferior part of the cartilaginous nasal septum is the site of Kiesselbach's plexus, which is responsible for over 90% of anterior epistaxis episodes. Kiesselbach's plexus represents an important watershed anastomosis between branches of the Internal Carotid Artery (ICA) and External Carotid Artery (ECA): (1) Anterior ethmoidal artery (from ophthalmic artery, ICA), (2) Sphenopalatine artery (terminal branch of maxillary artery, ECA), (3) Greater palatine artery (from maxillary artery, ECA), and (4) Superior labial artery (septal branch of facial artery, ECA). Note that the posterior ethmoidal artery does NOT contribute to Kiesselbach's plexus.",
  wrongExp: "• <strong>Option A (Anterior ethmoidal, posterior ethmoidal, sphenopalatine, and facial):</strong> The posterior ethmoidal artery supplies the posterosuperior quadrant of the nasal cavity and does not reach Little's area.<br>• <strong>Option C (Posterior ethmoidal, descending palatine, internal carotid, and maxillary):</strong> The main trunks of the internal carotid and maxillary arteries do not enter the nasal septum directly, and posterior ethmoidal is excluded.<br>• <strong>Option D (Sphenopalatine, infraorbital, ophthalmic, and ascending pharyngeal):</strong> The infraorbital, main ophthalmic, and ascending pharyngeal arteries do not participate in Kiesselbach's plexus."
