  ans: 1,
  exp: "Juvenile Nasopharyngeal Angiofibroma (JNA) is a histologically benign, locally aggressive, highly vascular non-capsulated tumor seen almost exclusively in adolescent males (androgen-dependent). It originates at the sphenopalatine foramen on the posterolateral nasal wall. A diagnostic hallmark on imaging is the Holman-Miller sign (antral sign), where the tumor pushes the posterior wall of the maxillary antrum anteriorly. OUTPATIENT BIOPSY IS STRICTLY CONTRAINDICATED due to the risk of life-threatening, uncontrollable hemorrhage. The diagnosis is made radiologically on contrast CT/MRI and angiography. Definitive treatment is complete surgical excision (endoscopic or open), preceded 24 to 48 hours earlier by superselective transarterial embolization (typically targeting the internal maxillary artery) to minimize intraoperative blood loss.",
  wrongExp: "• <strong>Option A (Urgent incisional biopsy in outpatient clinic):</strong> Performing a biopsy of JNA in the clinic can precipitate catastrophic, fatal hemorrhage because the tumor vessels are thin-walled channels lined by single-layer endothelium lacking a muscular tunica media.<br>• <strong>Option C (External beam radiotherapy is first-line for localized stages):</strong> Surgery is the first-line treatment; radiotherapy is reserved only for unresectable, recurrent intracranial tumors or critical cavernous sinus involvement due to risk of radiation-induced sarcomas.<br>• <strong>Option D (Arises from anterior septum; supplied by ICA):</strong> JNA originates at the margins of the sphenopalatine foramen and is supplied predominantly by branches of the external carotid artery (primarily the internal maxillary and ascending pharyngeal arteries)."
