  exp: "An Antrochoanal polyp (Killian's polyp) is a solitary, benign, non-allergic polyp that arises from the edematous mucosa of the maxillary sinus antrum. It characteristically grows out of the maxillary ostium (or more commonly an accessory ostium), enters the middle meatus, tracks posteriorly along the nasal floor, and passes through the posterior choana into the nasopharynx. It typically presents in children and young adults with unilateral nasal obstruction that is characteristically expiratory (as the polyp acts as a ball-valve against the choana). Definitive treatment is endoscopic sinus surgery (FESS) with complete removal of the antral attachment.",
  wrongExp: "• <strong>Option A (Ethmoidal polyps):</strong> Ethmoidal polyps are typically multiple, bilateral, grape-like clusters associated with allergy and asthma (Widal/Samter triad) seen in adults, presenting with bilateral inspiratory blockage.<br>• <strong>Option C (Rhinosporidiosis):</strong> Rhinosporidiosis forms a vascular, bleeding, strawberry-like granulomatous mass on the septum, not a pale, translucent choanal polyp.<br>• <strong>Option D (Encephalocele):</strong> Nasal encephaloceles originate from skull base defects (cribriform plate) and expand upon crying or jugular compression (Furstenberg test), appearing medial to the middle turbinate."
