  ans: 2,
  exp: "Le Fort III fracture (craniofacial dysjunction) involves complete separation of the facial skeleton from the cranial base. The fracture line passes horizontally through the nasofrontal suture, ethmoid bones (cribriform plate, causing CSF rhinorrhea), frontomaxillary suture, superior orbital fissure, lateral orbital wall, zygomaticofrontal suture, and zygomatic arches, ending through the pterygoid plates. Because the entire middle third of the face is detached from the skull base, testing for mobility results in movement of the nasal bridge, zygomas, and dental arch together as a single unit.",
  wrongExp: "• <strong>Option A (Le Fort I / Guerin's fracture):</strong> Le Fort I is a low horizontal fracture running above the alveolar ridge through the lower nasal septum, pyriform aperture, and pterygoid plates, wherein only the hard palate and teeth are mobile.<br>• <strong>Option B (Le Fort II / Pyramidal fracture):</strong> Le Fort II is a pyramidal fracture running through the nasofrontal suture, lacrimal bones, orbital floor, infraorbital rim, and pterygoid plates; the maxilla and nose move, but the zygomas remain attached to the cranium.<br>• <strong>Option D (Zygomaticomaxillary complex / tripod fracture):</strong> A tripod fracture involves isolated disruption of the zygoma at the infraorbital rim, zygomaticofrontal suture, and zygomatic arch, causing flattened cheek contour, not craniofacial dysjunction."
