  ans: 1,
  exp: "Thornwaldt's cyst (nasopharyngeal bursa cyst) is a congenital developmental anomaly located strictly in the midline of the posterior wall of the nasopharynx. Embryologically, as the primitive notochord ascends into the skull base, its caudal end can maintain a persistent fibrous or mucosal connection with the posterior pharyngeal wall endoderm. When the pharyngeal endoderm is pulled inward by the retracting notochord, a blind pouch lined by respiratory epithelium—the pharyngeal bursa of Luschka—is formed. Obstruction of the orifice of this bursa (secondary to infection or adenoidectomy scarring) results in fluid accumulation, producing a benign Thornwaldt's cyst.",
  wrongExp: "• <strong>Option A (Persistent patency of the second pharyngeal pouch):</strong> Persistence of the second pharyngeal pouch/cleft leads to branchial cleft cysts or fistulae located along the anterior border of the lower third of the sternocleidomastoid, not a midline nasopharyngeal cyst.<br>• <strong>Option C (Remnant of the craniopharyngeal canal from Rathke's pouch):</strong> Remnants of Rathke's pouch give rise to craniopharyngiomas or Rathke's cleft cysts in the sella/suprasellar region, not the lower posterior nasopharyngeal wall.<br>• <strong>Option D (Defective fusion of lateral nasal and maxillary processes):</strong> Defective fusion of facial processes causes cleft lip and oblique facial clefts, not mucosal bursa cysts."
