  exp: "Vocal cord nodules (Singer's or Screamer's nodules) are benign, bilateral, symmetrical mucosal swellings located at the junction of the anterior 1/3 and posterior 2/3 of the true vocal cords (the midpoint of the membranous vocal fold). This point experiences the maximum mechanical friction and vibratory impact during phonation. It is common in voice professionals (teachers, singers). Histology initially shows subepithelial edema, progressing to hyalinization and fibrosis. First-line management is strictly non-surgical: vocal hygiene, absolute voice rest, and professional speech/voice therapy. Microlaryngeal surgery is reserved only for recalcitrant, mature fibrotic nodules that fail extensive speech therapy.",
  wrongExp: "• <strong>Option A (Reinke's edema; Urgent bilateral laser cordectomy):</strong> Reinke's edema presents as diffuse, bilateral polypoid, water-bag-like swelling of the entire membranous vocal fold, typically seen in middle-aged postmenopausal smoking women.<br>• <strong>Option C (Vocal cord polyp; Immediate microflap excision):</strong> Vocal cord polyps are characteristically unilateral, larger, gelatinous/translucent or angiomatous, often originating from a single vocal trauma event.<br>• <strong>Option D (Laryngeal papilloma; Microdebrider excision):</strong> Papillomas appear as irregular, warty, cauliflower-like friable exophytic growths caused by HPV 6 and 11, not smooth symmetrical midpoint nodules."
