  exp: "Bilateral Recurrent Laryngeal Nerve (RLN) palsy is a critical post-thyroidectomy surgical emergency. The RLN supplies all intrinsic muscles of the larynx except the cricothyroid. Semon's law states that in progressive or acute organic lesions of the recurrent laryngeal nerve, the abductor fibers (supplying the posterior cricoarytenoid, PCA) are phylogenetically newer and far more vulnerable to trauma/compression than the adductor fibers; hence, abductor paralysis occurs first. Because the adductors (and the intact external-branch-innervated cricothyroid muscles) pull unopposed, both vocal cords adopt a paramedian position. This results in critical airway obstruction (severe inspiratory stridor requiring immediate reintubation or emergency tracheostomy), while the voice remains surprisingly intact because the cords can still approximate.",
  wrongExp: "• <strong>Option B (Bilateral superior laryngeal nerve palsy...):</strong> Isolated SLN palsy paralyzes the cricothyroid muscle, causing loss of vocal tension, inability to produce high-pitched notes, and a wavy, shortened cord margin, but never life-threatening airway stridor.<br>• <strong>Option C (Bilateral combined RLN and SLN complete paralysis...):</strong> In complete paralysis of both RLN and SLN (Wagner and Grossmann theory), all laryngeal muscles including the cricothyroid are paralyzed, causing the cords to rest in the intermediate/cadaveric position (loss of voice and aspiration, but a wider airway).<br>• <strong>Option D (Unilateral RLN palsy with contralateral neuropraxia):</strong> Unilateral palsy typically causes hoarseness with an adequate airway, not severe post-extubation stridor."
