  ans: 1,
  exp: "In the AJCC 8th edition Cancer Staging Manual, Depth of Invasion (DOI) was formally incorporated into the T-staging of Oral Cavity Carcinoma because DOI is the single most powerful predictor of occult cervical nodal metastasis and survival. The T-staging criteria for oral cavity are: (1) T1: Tumor ≤ 2 cm, with DOI ≤ 5 mm; (2) T2: Tumor ≤ 2 cm with DOI > 5 mm and ≤ 10 mm, OR Tumor > 2 cm and ≤ 4 cm with DOI ≤ 10 mm; (3) T3: Tumor > 4 cm regardless of DOI, OR ANY tumor with DOI > 10 mm (up to 20 mm); (4) T4: Moderately advanced (T4a: deep extrinsic muscle or cortical bone invasion) or very advanced (T4b: skull base/carotid). Here, although the surface diameter is 3.5 cm (which would be T2 by size alone), the DOI is 12 mm (>10 mm), which automatically upstages the primary tumor to T3.",
  wrongExp: "• <strong>Option A (T2; Because surface diameter is 2–4 cm regardless of DOI):</strong> This reflects the obsolete AJCC 7th edition system; ignoring a DOI of 12 mm leads to significant understaging.<br>• <strong>Option C (T4a; Any DOI exceeding 10 mm is T4a):</strong> T4a requires gross invasion of adjacent deep structures (mandibular or maxillary cortical bone, floor of mouth, skin, maxillary sinus), not an isolated DOI of 12 mm.<br>• <strong>Option D (T1; Depth of invasion is only considered in tumor grade):</strong> Histological grade (differentiation) is completely distinct from anatomical TNM stage; DOI is an integral parameter of T-staging in AJCC 8th edition."
